      "writtenOn": "2026-05-08T17:16:07Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Let me just quote myself from that thread:\n\n\u003e But actually, as I understand, the main reason for using READ_ONCE in such cases is to actually fully prevent TOCTOU/race issues like this. Without READ_ONCE (or more precisely, without volatile, which READ_ONCE relies on), we have no guarantee that the compiler will generate code that actually reads the value once, not multiple times. So, we may be using a temporarily variable to prevent TOCTOU, but the compiler is allowed to stubbornly implement that as loading the data from the host memory every time, instead of remembering it in a temporary storage. (And from what gemini told me, compilers may actually do that in practice, in order to save register space.)\n\nNow, the question is how likely it is that this particular `lapic_in_kernel(shared_vcpu)` usage here (with your patch, not before your patch) will cause such TOCTOU or race issues... Perhaps the answer is: theoretically possible (since without READ_ONCE, the compiler is still allowed to do any crazy things, as long as the behavior remains correct _within the compiler\u0027s single-threaded execution model_), but practically extremely unlikely (since we are only talking about a single `if` branch here).",
      "parentUuid": "84aee8c2_a7cd9ffb",
      "revId": "dd2385e52e95562dbc4292db4ef2b990d50b3968",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
